{:receipt :kotobase/harness-does-not-reproduce
 :date "2026-08-18"
 :status :open-question
 :severity "blocks iteration-05's seed; does NOT indicate a production regression"

 :what-was-being-checked
 "iteration-05's seed: re-run the phase bench and replace h1/h6/h7's
  :after-harness values, which were all taken against the old decode2. The
  bench was run twice at RELEASE, once from the h8 source and once from the
  reverted source, over the same cached production blocks and the same head."

 :the-two-runs
 [{:build "5aaf565bbd61c7c0" :source "h8"
   :whole-hydrate-cpu-ms 39242.581 :block-gets 148 :distinct 84 :bytes 56987151}
  {:build "809484c88ed86589" :source "reverted (main)"
   :whole-hydrate-cpu-ms 38062.356 :block-gets 148 :distinct 84 :bytes 56987151}]

 :attribution
 {:question "did h8 cause the duplicate gets and the CPU blowup?"
  :answer :no
  :evidence "the control is identical to three significant figures on every
             counter, and its 148/84/56,987,151 are the same numbers"
  :my-hypothesis-was-wrong
  "I had written that h8's extra promise hop per block probably broke
   `hot-datoms`' async batching and dropped the read back onto the O(N^2)
   trampoline, and that this explained both the duplicates and the CPU. The
   control refutes it. Recorded because the hypothesis was stated publicly
   before it was checked, and it was checked because it was stated."}

 :the-open-question
 {:documented "2026-08-17, same harness, same blocks, same head, release build:
               whole hydrate 2,201-2,232 cpu-ms, phase A 491-538, phase B
               1,386-1,473"
  :today "whole hydrate 38,062 cpu-ms, phase A 37,062, phase B 1,252"
  :phase-b-reproduces
  "1,252 against a documented 1,386-1,473 -- slightly lower, which is what the
   outer-string change should do. Phase B is fine."
  :phase-a-does-not "491-538 -> 37,062. About 70x."
  :why-that-matters-for-attribution
  "A measurement-wide artifact -- load, GC, a slower host -- would inflate BOTH
   phases. Only phase A moved, so it is not the environment."
  :and-production-is-unchanged
  "`wrangler tail` on the deployed Worker measures 2,058 cpu-ms per served read
   (n=10 median), and a probe today answered in 3.5 s wall. Production is not
   regressed. Whatever this is, it is in the harness or in how the harness
   drives the read, not in what kotobase.net serves."
  :first-suspect
  "`block_gets 148` against 84 distinct blocks, while the bench's OWN bucket
   counter reports `{:gets 85 :distinct 85 :misses 0}` for the same run. The two
   counters disagree because they count different things -- seam entries versus
   durable reads -- and 148 seam entries for 84 blocks means the read path
   re-entered the fetch 64 times and was answered from L1. Production reports 84
   with 0 duplicates. That difference is the thread to pull."}

 :what-was-NOT-done-because-of-it
 "h1, h6 and h7 keep their stale :after-harness values. Replacing them from a
  harness that no longer reproduces its own baseline, and that disagrees with
  production by 18x, would put a worse number in the loop than the one already
  labelled stale. The ranking's top entries therefore remain UPPER BOUNDS, as
  iteration-04 and -05 already said."

 :what-the-run-is-still-good-for
 "Everything measured WITHIN one run, where the harness is its own control:
  the phase A/B ratio, the per-collection parse costs (s3 collection 526 docs =
  399.7 cpu-ms of a 1,252 ms phase B; every other collection together ~38 ms),
  and the opaque-decode floor (3.1 cpu-ms to decode the seed without parsing
  values, against 888 ms with). Those are ratios, and the disputed factor
  cancels in them."}
